## 1. Introduction
Biomolecules are known to possess many vital functions for diverse biotechnological/energy/environmental applications (e.g., biosensors, gas storage, separation, catalysis, fluorescence, nonlinear optics, magnetism, and ion exchange). However, in the expansion of the applicability of biomolecular techniques, it is unavoidable to encounter significant challenges such as poor robustness, selectivity/specificity, operational stability, recovery, and reusability. To achieve enzyme immobilization on solid support, the first attempt was made as early as in 1916 (Nelson and Griffin, 1916). The invertase enzyme was used for the adsorption on charcoal and aluminum hydroxide. The invertase immobilization on the aforementioned substrates was not, nonetheless, seen to exert any effect on the activity of the enzyme.
In modern research, a list of advanced materials (e.g., metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), graphene, and molybdenum disulfide $(\mathrm{MOS}_{2})$ ) have been employed broadly as substrates/platforms for the immobilization of biomolecules (Das et al., 2017; Li et al., 2013a, 2013b;
Lian et al., 2017). Several approaches including surface immobilization, diffusion, in-situ encapsulation, biofunctionalization, and embedding biomolecules in materials have been developed and utilized for such applications (Allendorf et al., 2009; Buso et al., 2011; Kumar et al., 2016a). In this viewpoint, MOFs are considered as an excellent choice if used as the host matrix for immobilization of certain biomolecules (e.g., proteins and DNA) due to their excellent features (including stability, selectivity, porosity, crystalline structure, tunable pore size, and facile functionalization) (Hou et al., 2015). These features help eliminate or reduce the possibility of leaching of the embedded molecules due to enhanced stability (e.g., structure) and interactions between the host and biomolecules adsorbed (Chen et al., 2014). For example, micro enzymes such as MP-11 were incorporated into a mesoporous Tb-meso MOF (Chen et al., 2012a, b). This was possible due to the conformational changes adopted by these biomolecules during adsorption because of the dynamic and flexible structures of such hosting system. Likewise, many interesting investigations have been made regarding encapsulation of biomolecules into MOFs (Chen and Ma, 2016;
Gkaniatsou et al., 2017; Majewski et al., 2017). For such purposes, many strategies (e.g., surface immobilization, diffusion, in-situ encapsulation, and biofunctionalization) were invented and utilized for immobilizing biomolecules onto MOFs based on diverse mechanisms that include the covalent attachment of biomolecules and host-guest interactions (between the functional groups of biomolecules and pores of MOFs) (Liu et al., 2013a, 2014).
Because of diverse properties of MOFs, their sensing capacities have been explored in diverse respects, e.g., electrochemical, electromechanical, and optical sensing platforms (Kumar et al., 2017a; Lei et al., 2014). This contrasts with other novel materials in that their applications are generally confined to some specific sensing purposes. For instance, graphene and carbon nanotubes were exploited mainly for electrochemical sensing. Likewise, quantum dots (and some metal nanoparticles) with enhanced optical properties were used for the fluorescent and colorimetric determinations of analytes (Medintz et al., 2005; Nath and Chilkoti, 2002; Wu et al., 2013; Yang et al., 2015). Although various novel materials are identified to have large specific surface area, MOFs are different from others in that they share extra benefits of porous structure with diverse pore chemistry (Maurin et al., 2017). As the synthesis techniques of MOFs exert a significant control on their properties, one can attempt to tailor them as per the specific requirements for the final application (e.g., sensing) (Kitagawa, 2014). Moreover, MOFs can be conjugated with biomolecules through several techniques via in situ and post-synthetic processes (Hintz and Wuttke, 2014; Shen and Yan, 2015a).
The unique functionality of the MOFs facilitate the attachment of biomolecules via both covalent as well as non-covalent ways (Hintz and Wuttke, 2014; Wu et al., 2015b). However, such modification is not necessarily feasible for other materials. The MOFs have many sites for adsorption and conjugations which can be pores, metal centers, the hydrophobic rings of ligand, functional group of the ligands, and channels of the MOFs (Hintz and Wuttke, 2014; Liu et al., 2014; Raja et al., 2015; Zhu et al., 2013). It is however not easy for other nanomaterials to generate that many possible conjugation sites. In general, the metal nanoparticles (such as gold nanoparticles) rely on thiol chemistry, adsorption, and electrostatic interaction for the attachment of biomolecules (Himmelhaus and Takei, 2000; Karyakin et al., 2000; Warner and Hutchison, 2003). On the other hand, in case of carbonic nanostructures, it is more important to consider hydrophobic, electrostatic, and covalent conjugation than others (Kumar et al., 2017b; Kumar et al., 2017c; Lin et al., 2004; Yang et al., 2013a). As such, the unique and extraordinary characteristics of MOFs were demonstrated to be highly advantageous to enhance chances for efficient bioconjugation.
To date, many materials have been reported to serve as host materials for biomolecules (e.g., macroporous polymer films, mesoporous metal oxides, microporous zeolites, silica, and sol-gels). However, none of them have been identified to be more effective than MOFs in terms of chemical/operational stability and/or loading capacity (Battioni et al., 1996; Holland et al., 1998; Nestler and Severin, 2001). Such superiority of MOFs can be ascribable to their porous nature, exceptional surface areas, and possible interactions $\scriptstyle\mathtt{\backslash}\pi\mathtt{-}\pi$ interactions) between the functional (e.g., aromatic) groups of guest molecules and organic linker molecules present in MOFs (Kempahanumakkagari et al., 2018). These merits are useful to overcome some defects that other hosting materials suffer (e.g., the leakage of guest molecules); such occurrences, if occurring, can reduce the sensitivity during the sensing studies. It is difficult to expect these hosting interactions from other materials explored as potential hosts. The possible shortcomings of those materials (e.g., relative to MOFs) may be accounted for by their organic/inorganic moieties (e.g., less crystallinity) which can lead to the reduction in their porosity and surface areas (Chen and Ma, 2016). Moreover, another advantage of MOFs in encapsulation of biomolecule is that they can be synthesized in nanosized particles. As such, they can enhance their catalytic and/or sensing properties compared to other host materials with macro, meso, and micro porous nature. Moreover, MOFs can possess biomimetic catalytic properties similar to some features found in active sites of enzymes which can greatly help upgrade their sensing capacity towards biomolecules (Gkaniatsou et al., 2017). On the other hand, the major disadvantage of MOFs is yet found as their instability to moisture which is limiting their applicability. However, there is slow but steady progress to overcome these limitations with the introduction of some water-stable MOFs.
In this review, we focused on the various strategies used for biomolecular immobilization on MOFs with enzymes, antibodies, nucleic acids, and miscellaneous components for the development of various sensor applications (e.g., optical, electrochemical, biological, and other sensors). In addition, the importance of the structure of MOFs for immobilization to develop an innovative sensing platform is discussed. Consequently, this review paper should allow young researchers/ readers to acquire better insights into the structural importance of the host material (such as MOFs) for the immobilization of biomolecules for various (e.g., optical, electrochemical, and biological) sensing applications.
## 2. Prerequisites for biomolecule-embedded (or biointerfaces) arrangement
Biomolecules are subjected to several shortcomings such as fragile structures, the need for specific conditions for desired biological activity, and high reactivity towards non-specific components, and are often limited in terms of reusability and recovery when targeting for use in real/practical applications (Lian et al., 2017). To develop a biorecognition element for a sensor, a major task is to determine a suitable immobilization platform for biologically active materials (enzyme/antibody/nucleic acid). Such a platform should not hamper the performance of a material in terms of its stability and activity/sensitivity/ accuracy (Avnir et al., 2006). For example, the sensitivity of an electrochemical biosensor depends on the activity of the electrode surface and mass transfer ability of the analyte which in turn depends on interfacial properties of the immobilizing matrix and the biorecognition material (e.g., enzymes) (Patra et al., 2015). The unique features of MOFs can nonetheless facilitate the five different types of interactions i.e., covalent immobilization, adsorption, crosslinking, encapsulation, and matrix entrapment (Chen et al., 2010; Czaja et al., 2009; Kumar et al., 2016a; Liu, 2012; Lohe et al., 2011; Mehta et al., 2016). Basically, MOFs are a special category of materials with high surface-to-volume ratios and related structural diversities (Mehta et al., 2016). Moreover, many other advantageous properties as discussed above have allowed them to be used as novel/innovative platforms for sensing relative to common materials such as polycarbonate, sepharose, collagen, nylon alumina, silica, and zeolites (Bhardwaj et al., 2015; Bhardwaj et al., 2017; Deep et al., 2015; Kumar et al., 2016a; Mehta et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2015).
Among numerous advanced materials, ideal host material for biomolecules (e.g., through adsorption) should exhibit the following properties: (i) high surface area with suitable porosity: a high specific surface area favors higher immobilization of biorecognition molecules, whereas smaller cavities should facilitate the diffusion of analytes and reaction products, (ii) biocompatibility, (iii) operational stability, and (iv) rich-surface chemical tunability. These features are extremely advantageous in anchoring diverse biorecognition molecules on the host matrix to develop specific devices for monitoring diverse analytes (Deep et al., 2015; Kumar et al., 2016a; Wang et al., 2015). For example, high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capturing efficiency was achieved by conjugating CuMOFs with carbonic anhydrase (CA) molecules via physical as well as chemical attachments (Zhang et al., 2017). The embedding of enzymes into MOFs was also reported to enhance the optimal separation performance of MOFs. For instance, the enzyme-embedded MOFs demonstrated 20.9 fold (i.e., selectivity of 165.5) enhancements in the selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , when compared with pristine MOF materials. An enhanced of 3.2-fold in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance on enzyme-embedded MOFs was attributed to the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions (Zhang et al., 2017). Nevertheless, biorecognition molecules attached via simple surface adsorption on a host matrix are susceptible to leaching throughout the reaction process due to the lack of any precise interactions (Wang et al., 2015). In order to overcome the leaching of biomolecules, the use of host material with a three-dimensional matrix for their entrapment was demonstrated to be very beneficial to plays a crucial role in targeted applications (Wang et al., 2015). In terms of real applications, the main necessities for an innovative sensing platform to support materials should be advantageous to include high surface area, ease of functionalization, biocompatibility, inertness, resistance to extreme environments, and cost effective (Deep et al., 2015; Kumar et al., 2016a; Wang et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2014b). However, it was also noticed that the entrapment of molecules may reduce the biomolecular associability on the surface of the electrode and mass transfer ability of the analyte which can decrease the sensitivity of the sensor (especially, in case of electrochemical sensing systems) (Bhardwaj et al., 2015; Bhardwaj et al., 2017; Kumar et al., 2016a; Mehta et al., 2016).
In the coming section, various immobilization methods in/on MOFs are critically discussed with respect to the type of biomolecules such as antibodies, proteins, genetic materials, and miscellaneous biomolecules for the development of innovative sensing platforms or solid supports for desired sensing applications.
## 3. Methods to conjugate biomolecule with MOFs
The large specific surface area and functionality of MOFs allow several types of bio-conjugation strategies. Several methods have been employed for immobilization of biorecognition molecules in/on MOFs for the development of sensing platforms. The common biorecognition molecules exploited for the development of sensing platforms are enzymes, antibodies, peptides, nucleic acids, and amino acids. The molecules can be immobilized onto MOFs either by de novo methods, surface immobilization, diffusion, in-situ encapsulation, biofunctionalization, and many other strategies, which can increase the operational and structural stability of biomolecules for applications in various fields.
## 3.1. Enzyme embedded MOFs
Enzymes can be immobilized onto MOFs either by de novo methods which involve arranging the MOFs around the enzymes or by a postsynthetic method in which enzymes are immobilized onto a pre-existing MOF. Both methods are reported to enhance the operational stability of the enzymes for various applications (Hou et al., 2015). Nevertheless, the combined effects of the crystallite features and size of MOFs (as an immobilizing matrix) can control the diffusion of substrates during specific applications in aqueous/non-aqueous solutions.
## 3.1.1. Enzyme embedded MOFs by the de novo method
The de novo method involves in situ or one pot synthesis of enzymeMOF composites. In this protocol, the larger enzyme molecules (compared to the apertures or channel size of the MOFs) can be immobilized into the cavities of MOFs. Enzyme-MOF composites prepared through this method are also beneficial to stabilize the tertiary structure of an enzyme. In some cases, auxiliary stabilizers were required for an enzyme during the preparation of enzyme-MOF composites (Gu et al., 2012). In a typical example of de novo synthesis, a composite of graphene, glucose oxidase $(\mathbf{GOx})$ , and MOF was prepared (Guo et al., 2012). Briefly, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of presynthesized PVP (Polyvinylpyrrolidone) functionalized graphene $(0.05\mathrm{~mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ , $95\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of paraphenylenediamine $(0.15\mathrm{M})$ , $375\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ (0.038 M), and $10\mathrm{mg}$ of GOx were added to a vessel and stirred on magnetic stirrer at room temperature to achieve with a color change of the solution. The obtained precipitates were separated by centrifugation and washed with ethanol several times followed by dispersion in water (Guo et al., 2012). The Pt (II) or Pt (IV) of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ can coordinate with nitrogen atoms of the paraphenylenediamine ligand to give nano colloids in the synthesis. These nano colloids cover the surface of the graphene sheets uniformly. This was evidenced by atomic force microscopy (AFM), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) studies of the composites by these authors. The uniform surface coverage of graphene sheets with Pt-based nano colloids helped in loading more GOx molecules which helped in increase in sensitivity in electrochemical sensing applications (Guo et al., 2012). Similarly, a de novo approach was used for the size-selective covering of catalase in ZIF-90 (Fig. 1) (Shieh et al., 2015). This enzyme-embedded MOF played a significant role as a biocatalyst. The de novo approach facilitated the formation of MOFs with smaller pore size than catalase enzyme (Shieh et al., 2015). The pore size of ZIF-90 is $1\mathrm{nm}$ while that of the catalase is $10\mathrm{nm}$ . The smaller pore size of ZIF-90 facilitates the size-selectivesheltering for catalase in order to increase the tolerance against protease and also minimizes the leaching. This is because the interactions between MOF and enzymes are directly responsible for determining the extent of leaching. In the present composites, the interactions between ZIF-90 and enzymes are marginal (Van der walls forces) due to their small size (Shieh et al., 2015). The present de novo approach utilizes water as the solvent to offer the milder reaction conditions as a means to avoid organic solvent. The use of organic solvent, if used on the other hand, should denature or decrease the activity of enzyme present in the composites. The de novo approach not only prevented leaching of enzyme from substrate, but also enhanced the possibilities for the selection of enzymes and ZIF-90 for diverse functional applications (Shieh et al., 2015). Likewise, many other reports on the development of enzyme-embedded MOFs by the de novo method for an innovative sensing platform are discussed in Section 4.
In the aforementioned approach, the pore size of the MOFs is generally smaller than that of the enzyme to be embedded. This not only helps in leaching out of the enzymes from MOFs but also enables to select diverse MOFs and enzymes, making the method suitable for diverse functional applications. In this respect, the ZIF-90 embedded with protease and catalase was discussed in earlier section. The two enzymes in this study were protease and catalase used in textile industry. The protease is proteolytic enzyme used for degumming of silk. In contrast. Catalase is peroxide decomposing enzyme used in waste water treatment (Shieh et al., 2015). The existence of both the enzymes (i.e., protease and catalase) in solution is not possible because the former can cleave the peptide bonds of the later (Shieh et al., 2015).
## 3.1.2. Post synthetic modification
Post synthetic modification involves immobilization of enzymes on pre-synthesized MOFs by a physical/chemical process. Fundamentally, physical immobilization involves the simple impregnation of the MOF and the target enzyme in the solution phase. In contrast, in case of chemical modification, the immobilization of biomolecules was achieved by the chemical reaction between the free functional groups of MOFs and enzymes with the aid of coupling agents/cross-linkers. In a typical example of chemical modification, the MOFs such as MIL-33 and MIL-101 were functionalized with the glucose oxidase $(\mathbf G\mathbf{Ox})$ enzyme (Tudisco et al., 2016). The free $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups of the MOFs (MIL-33 and MIL-101) were converted to COOH groups and utilized for covalent immobilization of GOx with its free $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups. The COOH groups of the MOFs were activated by treating with N-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS) to obtain NHS-MOF (ester). The NHS-MOF was finally covalently linked with the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups of the GOx by the formation of amide bond. In the first step conjugation, the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ group of the MOF was converted to –COOH using glutaric anhydride. Subsequently, an N-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS) terminated MOF was prepared by treating the MOF with NHS and 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide (EDC). Finally, the NHS-MOF was treated with GOx to generate MOFGOx in the presence of 1,4-dioxane. The zeta potentials (ζ) were measured to determine the electrostatic charges on the MOFs during progression of the reaction. The zeta potential values were found to be positive (14.2 and $32.5\mathrm{mV}$ for $N_{\mathrm{H_{2}-M I L}-53}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\cdot}101$ , respectively) for the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ –MOFs due to the presence of metal ions and protonated amino groups. However, the $\zeta$ of COOH-MOFs $(-9.9$ and $-23.1\:\mathrm{mV}$ for COOH–MIL-53 and COOH–MIL-101, respectively) and GOx-MOFs $(-21.9\$ and $-41.0\mathrm{mV}$ for GOx–MIL-53 and GOx–MIL-101, respectively) were observed to be negative due to surface charges of carboxylic groups and GOx. The concentration of immobilized enzyme was also altered with the type of MOF used. For instance, the effective enzyme concentration on the MOFs was found to be 18 and $17\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ for MIL-53 and MIL-101, respectively.
The immobilization of enzyme on the surface of MOFs is often found to hamper the enzyme activity. In case of immobilization of GOx on MOFs, 46 and $44\%$ of enzyme activity was preserved (when compared with the activity of the free enzyme) after their attachment with MIL-53 and MIL-101, respectively. The probable reason for the decreased enzyme activity was the loss of enzyme structure during functionalization (Tudisco et al., 2016). However, the protocol discussed above involves multi steps compared to de nova approach with single step. The latter proceeds as an in situ process where precursors of MOF as well as enzyme are added together to obtain MOF enzyme composites. In post synthetic modification (PSM) approach, pre-synthesized MOF was taken and enzyme was covalently bonded to particular functional group of MOF through several steps. In the PSM, MOFs with free $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups were selected in order to couple with GOx (Tudisco et al., 2016). Then $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group was converted to COOH. Accordingly, MOF with free COOH group can effectively reduce the synthesis steps relative to the one with free $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group. Hence, through the selection of the COOH-MOF, the first step in the above discussed protocol (i.e., conversion of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MOF to COOH-MOF using glutaric anhydride) can be avoided. Such simplification of chemical synthesis procedures improved the yields of final products which was very essential in multistep synthesis. The above discussed GOx-MOFs produced by PSM nevertheless exhibited low recyclability relative to other reported GOx-MOFs composites in catalytic or sensing studies (Cheng et al., 2016; Lian et al., 2016; Patra et al., 2015).
## 3.2. Antibody embedded MOFs
Apart from enzymes, antibodies were also incorporated with MOFs to increase the specificity and selectivity of sensors. Antibodies immobilized on MOFs have been used in diverse sensing applications (such as against diseases or pollutants) and targeted drug delivery (Chang et al., 2005; Fernandez-Fernandez et al., 2011; Xu et al., 2011; Zhao et al., 2004). Generally, PSM was employed for the synthesis of antibody conjugated MOFs. Special control of the reaction conditions (such as pH and temperature) was necessary during the conjugation of these biomolecules. Hence, several different buffers were utilized to maintain the reaction parameters (such as pH) of the reaction mixture during antibody conjugation. The frequently used buffers were 2-(Nmorpholino) ethanesulfonic acid (MES), phosphate buffered saline (PBS), phosphate buffer (PB), and tris(hydroxymethyl) aminomethane (Tris) (Chang et al., 2005; Fernandez-Fernandez et al., 2011; Xu et al., 2011; Zhao et al., 2004). Out of all the post synthesis modification methods used for attachment of antibodies to MOFs, pendent group activation was most often explored due to its large availability and easy activation. In a typical example of pendent group activation, the pendent group of Zn-MOF $\mathtt{(Z n_{4}O}$ $\mathrm{(BDC)}_{3}$ . DMF) was activated for the covalent conjugation of anti-bovine serum albumin (anti-BSA) (Kumar et al., 2014). Activation of the pendent group was achieved using $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{~M~}}$ MES buffer and the EDC cross-linker. During covalent conjugation, PBS buffer was used to maintain the pH of the reaction mixture. Likewise, anti-parathion was immobilized on a MOF of [Cd(atc) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (NMOF1) (Kumar et al., 2016b). The EDC-NHS cross linkers were used to couple carboxylic and primary amine groups of MOF/biomolecule for the conjugation. More specifically, the carboxylic pendant group of MOFs and amine group of antibodies were used for the synthesis of MOF-antibody conjugate (Kumar et al., 2014, 2016b). The interaction between carboxyl group and EDC resulted in the formation of intermediate, i.e., O-acylisourea (Liang et al., 2004; Sam et al., 2009). As the produced intermediate is very active, it can be readily hydrolyzed to form carboxyl group in the absence of amine group. As the hydrolysis of such intermediates can decrease conjugation efficiency, the use of NHS is beneficial to avoid the hydrolysis.
The interaction of NHS with the intermediate components can lead to the formation of NHS ester, which can serve as active site for amine. The NHS ester was thus found to readily react with the nearby amine group to form amide bond (Liang et al., 2004; Sam et al., 2009). The EDC-NHS was exploited further for the conjugation between amine functionalized MOFs and carboxylic groups of antibodies. For instance, the EDC-NHS cross-linker was used in MES buffer $\mathrm{(pH}5.0)$ for covalent attachment of anti-atrazine on thin films of Cu-MOFs for sensing of atrazine (Bhardwaj et al., 2015). The use of linker molecules can also be avoided by using direct conjugation methods. In this regard, one step immobilization of a half fragmented monoclonal S. aureus antibody on ZIF-8 hybrid structures was achieved by reducing the disulfide bond between the heavy and light chains of the antibody (Yim et al., 2017). The free sulfur group of the antibody was then interacted with the $\scriptstyle2\ n^{2+}$ ion of the MOF resulting in a $z\mathrm{n}{-}s$ bond. PB with $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ was used for the antibody conjugation. Non-specific binding was prevented by incubating the antibody-conjugated MOFs with $0.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ casein and $0.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Tween 20 surfactant (Yim et al., 2017). The half fragmentation of antibodies, while being readily chemisorbed on the substrate surface, helped retain immunological activity of antibodies (Sharma and Mutharasan, 2013). The half fragmented antibodies can be easily prepared with the help of reduction with tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP). The TCEP reduced antibodies can then be easily chemisorbed on the substrate. The main problem with the common antibodies immobilization methods often lies in the accurate orientation of antibodies for interaction with antigens. The presence of thiol groups on TCEP reduced antibodies was helpful for their oriented attachment on the sensing platform. As such, the oriented attachment of antibodies directly contributed to the enhancement of the sensor's sensitivity by increasing available active sites on the sensing substrate (Sharma and Mutharasan, 2013).
The status of conjugation between MOFs and antibodies can be investigated more deliberately with the aid of several spectroscopic techniques such as UV–Vis and FTIR spectroscopies. For instance, the analysis of anti-BSA conjugated MOFs confirmed the completion of bioconjugation through the shift in the band of UV–Vis spectra and appearance of amide bond (i.e., formed due to EDC-HNS coupling of carboxyl and amine group) (Kumar et al., 2014). The band in the UV–Vis spectrum was also shifted from 406 to $423\ \mathrm{nm}$ with the conjugation of antibodies on MOF. The shifting of the absorption band was attributed to the increased particle size and reduced surface charge on MOFs (Kumar et al., 2014) (Kumar et al., 2014). Likewise, the absorption band of NMOF-1 was shifted from 305 to $350\mathrm{nm}$ after the attachment of anti-parathione (Kumar et al., 2016b). Likewise, the additional peak of amide bond in the FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{BTC@SiO}_{2}$ was observed at $1640\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after their conjugation (Bhardwaj et al., 2015).
## 3.3. Nucleic acid embedded MOFs
Nucleic acids are a class of biomolecules designated as deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and ribonucleic acid (RNA) depending upon the type of sugar moiety present. The incorporation of these biomolecules into MOFs resulted in the development of highly specific biosensors for several diseases and microorganisms (Zhang et al., 2014; Zhu et al., 2013). Nucleic acid embedded MOFs can be prepared by covalent as well as non-covalent interactions. A dysprosium-based MOF was used for the non-covalent conjugation of single-stranded DNA (ss-DNA) for sensing of a complementary Ebola virus RNA sequence (Zhang et al., 2014; Zhu et al., 2013). The benzene groups of fluorescent carboxyfluorescein (FAM) labeled ss-DNA strongly interacted with organic ligand molecules of the MOF via π-π stacking, electrostatic interactions, and H-bonding (Qin et al., 2016). The ss-DNA conjugation was achieved by keeping them with the MOF (Qin et al., 2016). Similarly, an amine functionalized UiO-66 MOF $(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ was used for the immobilization of FAM labeled ss-DNA (Zhang et al., 2014; Zhu et al., 2013). For the synthesis of ss-DNA conjugated MOFs, both the MOF and probe DNA were incubated in Tris-HCl buffer at room temperature (Zhang et al., 2014). The MOFs were sonicated in distilled water or buffer before the addition of biomolecules to the reaction mixture (Zhang et al., 2014; Zhu et al., 2013). The $\uppi$ -electrons system of organic linkers of MOFs is suspected for the ss-DNA conjugation on MOFs. By keeping ss-DNA with MOFs in suitable buffer media led to the formation of $\scriptstyle{\pi-\pi}$ and hydrogen bond between MOFs and ss-DNA. The π- electrons system of UiO-66 MOF and aromatic nucleotide bases of ssDNA was attributed to the interaction between both the structures. In another report, a mechanical shaker was used for the conjugation of DNA with UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N}_{3}$ . The MOF and DNA were shaken for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an aqueous solution (Morris et al., 2014). Oligonucleotides have also been covalently attached to MOFs. In a typical example of covalent attachment, dibenzylcyclooctyne (DBCO) functionalized DNA was conjugated with an azide functionalized MOF (i.e., $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{3};$ via a click reaction (Morris et al., 2014). The triple bond of DBCO reacted with the ${\bf N}_{3}$ group of MOF to form covalent coupling between MOF and DNA. The click chemistry reaction for the conjugation of DNA with UiO-66 is promising because of high efficiency even under mild conditions (Morris et al., 2014). The UiO-66 nano-MOF (NMOF) was conjugated with a FAM labeled nucleic acid. The NMOF was blended with a dye labeled probe biomolecule in Tris-HCl buffer (pH 7.5) (Wu et al., 2015a). He et al. (2014a) used multiple drug resistance (MDR) genes and gene-silencing siRNAs for bioconjugation with UiO-66 NMOF via surface coordination. The si-RNA was attached on a cisplatin-UiOMOF by simple mixing. The mass ratio of cisplatin:siRNA used for synthesis of nucleic acid modified MOF was 4.5:1 (He et al., 2014a). The siRNA was immobilized on the MOF surface via multiple coordination bonds formed between the phosphate residue present on the siRNA backbone and $z\mathrm{r}$ vacant sites on the MOF surface (He et al., 2014a). The labeling of nucleic acids with the dye was used to analyze the conjugation of DNA/RNA with the MOFs. For instance, DNA functionalized with TAMRA dye was characterized for the conjugation with MOF by using $540\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength of light in confocal microscopy (Morris et al., 2014). To this end, the measurement of zeta potential was used to determine the efficiency of conjugation between MOF and DNA. In case of conjugation between DNA and UiO-66 MOF, a decrease in the zeta potential was accompanied from −27 to $-41\mathrm{mV}$ .
## 3.4. Miscellaneous material embedded MOFs
Over the past few years, nucleobases have been widely utilized for the synthesis of bio-MOFs. In such bio-MOFs, the porosity can be altered by varying the length of the aliphatic chain of organic ligand molecules associated with metal ions (Li et al., 2013a). Li et al. (2013a) used different cobalt metal ion precursors (differentiated by the carbonic chain i.e., acetate, propionate, butyrate, and valerate attached with metal ion) along with adenine for the synthesis of bio-MOFs with diverse properties. In all cases, a $1{:}1\mathrm{~M~}$ ratio was used for the preparation of black octahedral crystals. The MOF with a smaller aliphatic chain exhibited the highest Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $1148\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . On the other hand, the MOF with smaller aliphatic chain demonstrated pore volume of $0.45\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . In contrast, the MOF synthesized with cobalt valerate exhibited the lowest BET surface area $(17\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and pore volume $(0.035\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ . The metal salt and adenine solution was frozen with liquid nitrogen and heated ( $.130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{{h}}^{\cdot}$ ) in an oven during the synthesis of MOFs. Moreover, the MOFs developed with the in-situ exchange of ligands also exhibited different pore volumes (Li et al., 2013b). Li et al. (2013b) synthesized different analogues variants of bio-MOF-100 (i.e., bio-MOF 101 to bio-MOF 103) by replacing biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) ligands differently with three ligands, i.e., 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate (NDC), azobenzene-4,4′-dicarboxylate (ABDC), and $2^{\prime}$ -amino-1,1′:4,1″-terphenyl$^{4,4^{\prime\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate $({\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{TPDC}})$ , respectively. The bio-MOF-102 displayed the maximum pore volume of $4.36\mathrm{cm}^{3}/8$ . The ligand exchange method can be used to synthesize MOFs with the highest porosity. In addition, the ligand exchange method can also be used to synthesize non-interpenetrated MOFs and thermodynamically unfavorable MOFs (Li et al., 2013b).
The BPDC linker was used to interconnect hydrothermally synthesized Zn-adenine building units for the synthesis of MOFs $(\mathrm{Zn_{8}(a d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O\cdot2M e_{2}N H_{2}})$ (Liu et al., 2016). During hydrothermal synthesis, the reaction mixture was heated to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 day in a Teflon-lined autoclave. The europium ions were encapsulated within the MOF via post synthesis modification (cation exchange). The reaction between zinc salt and adenine led to the formation of compressed structures having unapproachable pores (An et al., 2009b). The porosity of the material was enhanced by the addition of biphenyldicarboxylic acid which produced single crystal structures of bio-MOF-1. The crystal structure of bio-MOF-1 was composed of zinc-adeninate columnar secondary building units (SBUs) with octahedral cages (An et al., 2009b; Shen and Yan, 2015a). Apart from europium ions, other lanthanide cations such as ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{S m}^{3+}$ , and ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ were also encapsulated with bio-MOFs to enhance their photophysical properties (An et al., 2011). Similarly, several other reports have also been made to describe the hydrothermal synthesis of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -adenine based bio-MOF (An et al.,
2012, 2009a; An and Rosi, 2010; An et al., 2011; Bohrman and Carreon, 2012; Joarder et al., 2015; Li et al., 2014, 2013b; Li and Rosi, 2013; Liu, 2012; Liu et al., 2015; Shen and Yan, 2015b). Other metal ions such as $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ were also explored for the synthesis of bio-MOFs using adenine. The hydrothermal approaches used for the synthesis of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ based bio-MOFs are similar to the Zn based bio-MOFs. In a typical example of a Co based bio-MOF, $0.45\mathrm{mM}$ cobalt butyrate/cobalt valerate was mixed with $1.35\mathrm{mM}$ of adenine in a Schlenk tube and evaporated at 200 mTorr after freezing with liquid nitrogen. The grey colored powder was collected after heating at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ (Xie et al., 2014). An isoreticular series of Co-based MOFs was synthesized by varying the aliphatic chain as either an acetate, propionate, butyrate, or valerate (Li et al., 2013a). Other metal ions were also explored for the synthesis of bio-MOFs such as Cu and Cd (García-Terán et al., 2004; Yang et al., 2007).
Nucleobase derived bio-MOFs are dominated by adenine based bioMOFs. This is due to five main factors: 1) the adenine molecule has several coordination modes, 2) the rigid structure of adenine allows the formation of permanent porosity in the MOFs, 3) adenine offers five metal binding sites (N1, N3, N7, and N9 imino nitrogens, and N6 associated with the amino group), 4) adenine allows an extended range of metal ion binding pattern, and 5) adenine has the ability to adsorb small molecules such as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (An et al., 2009b; Imaz et al., 2011; Salam and Aoki, 2000).
The MOFs were also modified with peptide molecules to enhance their sensing efficiency. A $Z\mathbf{n}$ and Cd based MOF-peptide framework was synthesized by varying the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the reaction mixture. Glypeptide and $\mathrm{Cd(NO_{3})_{2}.4H_{2}O/Z n(N O_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ were used for the synthesis of peptide-MOF, and at $\mathtt{p H}6$ bio-MOFs exhibited $\mathrm{\small{IM}(G l y G l y)_{2}]2H_{2}O}$ (M: $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ or $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ structure. At higher $\mathfrak{p H}$ (i.e., 9) bio-MOFs with the structure $\mathrm{[Cd(GlyGly)_{2}]H_{2}O}$ were formed (Takayama et al., 1996). Similarly, several peptide-MOFs (e.g., $\mathrm{[Zn(GlyThr)_{2}]2H_{2}O_{3}}$ $\mathrm{[Cd(AlaThr)_{2}]}$ $4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\left[\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{A l a A l a})_{2}\right]$ , and $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GlyAla})_{2}])$ were also synthesized for diverse applications (Imaz et al., 2011; Lee et al., 2007; Rabone et al., 2010; Ueda et al., 2004).
## 4. Biomolecule-embedded MOFs as an innovative sensing platform
Biomolecule-embedded MOFs have been synthesized for an innovative sensing platform. In this section, various types of sensor applications including optical, electrochemical, biological, and miscellaneous platforms are critically discussed based on recent investigation of biomolecule-embedded MOFs. In addition, the importance of the MOF structures for immobilization to establish an innovative sensing platform is thoroughly discussed, which should be helpful for researchers in this emerging field in many respects.
## 4.1. Optical sensing platform
For the past few decades, optical (colorimetric and fluorometric) sensors have been extensively used for detection of environmental and biologically important target analytes such as heavy metal ions (Hg, As, Pb, Cd, and Cr), glucose, L-cysteine, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , dopamine, and ascorbic acid (Chakrapani et al., 2014; Karthiga and Anthony, 2013). These sensors are advantageous due to their simple color change in the presence of an analyte which can be detected by the naked eye. At present, various effective spectroscopic (such as inductively coupled plasma emission spectroscopy and atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS)) and chromatographic techniques are used extensively in the analytical laboratories. The major drawbacks associated with such techniques are difficulties in the operation of sophisticated instrumentation and associated requirement of skilled operators. On the contrary, optical sensor systems are simple, cost effective, and portable, allowing in field analysis devices to be designed due to their less sophisticated instrumentation. Recently, biomolecule-embedded and enzyme mimic MOFs have been reported for the development of a variety of optical sensors for diverse analytes.
Cytochrome (Cyt c) protein embedded MOF (ZIF (zeolitic imidazolate framework)-8) composites (Cyt c/ZIF-8) have been prepared by a coprecipitation method and used for fluorometric sensing of hydrogen peroxide and explosive organic peroxides (i.e., methyl ethyl ketone peroxide (MEKP) and tertiary butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP)) (Lyu et al., 2014). These authors used $N$ -acetyl-3,7-dihydroxyphenoxazine (Amplex Red) as the substrate in these sensing studies. The Cyt c immobilized ZIF-8 catalyzed oxidation of Amplex Red to produce fluorescently active resorufin, and the emission of resorufin was measured to detect peroxides. The intensity of emission was enhanced with increasing the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from 0 to $80~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . As determined by the standard bicinchoninic acid (BCA) protein assay kit, $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ Cyt c was found to be present in c/ZIF-8 composite. Similarly, the protein encapsulation yield on the MOF was calculated as $82.3\%$ . The Cyt ${\bf c}/$ ZIF-8 composite demonstrated rapid and sensitive detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ MEKP, and TBHP.
To evaluate the efficiency of the developed platform, the performance of Cyt c/ZIF-8 was compared with free Cyt c. Free Cyt c demonstrated very slow reaction rates and no detectable signals for peroxides when compared with Cyt c/ZIF-8. The immobilization of Cyt c on ZIF-8 resulted into 10 times enhancement in its peroxidase activity/ apparent activity (activity evaluated by Michaelis−Menten kinetics). The Michaelis−Menten kinetics parameters (i.e., $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{m a x}}$ values) for embedded Cyt c were calculated as $2\mathrm{mM}$ and $200\upmu\mathrm{M/min}$ , respectively, whereas these values were $15\mathrm{mM}$ and $80\upmu\mathrm{M/min}$ for free Cyt c. The decreased $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ value for the embedded protein was due to an increase in substrate binding affinity. This increased affinity may be due to incubation of the protein in methanol, which resulted in a conformational change exposing the heme groups (Yang et al., 2013b). An additional reason for the increased substrate binding affinity was the microenvironment created by the immobilization carrier, i.e., ZIF-8. As ZIF-8 exhibited almost negligible catalytic performance in sensing of peroxides, the possibility of false results was minimal (Lyu et al., 2014). The same immobilization matrix (i.e., ZIF-8) was used for synthesizing bovine hemoglobin (BHb) MOF composites $(\mathsf{Z I F-8@B H b})$ by the de novo procedure. The developed ZIF- ${8@\mathrm{BHb}}$ platform was tested as a colorimetric sensor for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and phenol (Yin et al., 2016). The composites exhibited excellent peroxidase mimic activities. The $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ parameters for peroxidase activity for $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) oxidation of the protein and composites alone were 0.28 and $1.02\mathrm{mM}$ with ZIF- ${8@\mathrm{BHb}}$ and free BHb, respectively. This indicates the greater affinity of TMB with the composites than with free BHb. Similarly, the $\mathtt{V_{m}}$ values calculated for TMB oxidation were 6.45 and $1.14\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}$ for the composite embedded protein and free protein, respectively, which demonstrated that the rate of reaction was faster with the composite embedded protein. The protein embedded ZIF- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}8@\mathrm{BHb}$ composite was also tested for colorimetric determination of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and phenols in both biological and environmental samples. The ZIF- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}8@\mathbf{BHb}$ composite displayed signals which could be detected by the naked eye for sensing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and phenol (Yin et al., 2016).
Likewise, the de novo process was exploited for the development of a multi-enzyme mimic system, composed of GOx and magnetic ZIF-8. The developed mZIF- $8@\mathbf{GOx}$ composite was investigated for the colorimetric sensing of glucose (Hou et al., 2015). The present MOF (mZIF8) exhibits higher catalytic peroxidase activity compared to that of the horseradish peroxidase (HRP) enzyme. The activity was calculated by measuring the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values of mZIF-8 and HRP. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and OPD (orthophenylene diamine) were used as substrates during the activity studies. The $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values for mZIF-8 with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and OPD as substrates were 2.42 and 0.62, respectively. Whereas, the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values for HRP with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and OPD as substrates were 3.7 and 7.3, respectively. The lower $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values of mZIF-8 revealed its higher affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and OPD compared to HRP. Besides the peroxidase activity, mZIF-8 serves as a good immobilizing matrix by increasing the operational stability of GOx. mZIF8/GOx can withstand higher temperatures $\left(30{-}60\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ and a wider range of pH (3–9) compared to free GOx, which loses its catalytic activity beyond a certain temperature $(50^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and pH region of 6–7. The authors utilized the magnetic properties of mZIF-8 for reusability purposes. After the reaction, a magnet was used to separate the synthesized composite from the solution, which was reused further. The recycled materials exhibited the same catalytic properties even after 12 cycles. Finally, the composite was tested for the measurement of glucose in urine samples (Hou et al., 2015).
Metal-adeninate frameworks (bio-MOFs) are gaining interest in sensing applications due to many merits (e.g., enduring microporosity, high surface area, stable chemical structure, and the presence of basic biomolecular building units). A series of adeninate embedded MOFs (bioMOF-1) have been prepared by encapsulating different lanthanide ions (e.g., ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ) via postsynthetic cation exchange method (Shen and Yan, 2015a). These bio-MOFs with luminescent properties have then been exploited for sensing of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in aqueous or DMF solution. In the same way, ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ - $\upbeta$ -diketonate loaded adeninate embedded anionic MOFs exhibited color variations in the presence of organic amines, e.g., methyl amine (Shen and Yan, 2015b). This anionic bio-MOF $\mathrm{(Zn_{8}(a d)_{4}(B P D C)_{6}O_{2}M e_{2}N H_{2}};$ $\mathtt{B P D C}=$ biphenyl-4,40-dicarboxylate, $\mathbf{Ad}=$ adeninate) was considered as a platform of interest as the cationic guest molecules can take part in facile exchange with other exogenous cationic species. In addition, $\upbeta$ -diketonate (thenoyltrifluoroacetonate) which should act as the second sensitizing ligand can effectually sensitize the luminescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ -doped MOFs.
All of the methods discussed above used physical immobilization of enzymes onto the MOFs. However, a new approach using covalent attachment of an GOx enzyme with the MOF has been also reported and used for the colorimetric determination of glucose ([). The synthetic details of this process are given in Section 3.1.2. Two MOFs $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ -MIL53 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101) were chemically modified for GOx conjugation and tested for glucose sensing. These two composites could be reused many times and their activity was retained. The analytical characteristics of the optical biosensors based on the MOFs are listed in Table 1.
Based on the evaluation of the optical sensing properties (as discussed in this section), it is possible to draw the conclusion that the MOF-biomolecule composites can be used as efficient sensing materials. Some MOFs in composite materials exhibited good loading capacity (e.g., about $80\%$ ) with enhanced sensing properties relative to free enzyme/biomolecules (Lyu et al., 2014). The reason for such observation can be accounted for by the microenvironment generated by MOF structures in the composites. In some cases, MOFs were also found to have the enhanced thermal stability (e.g., compared to their free enzyme counterparts) which also led to increases in the operational stability of their composites. Similarly, composites with enzymes anchored covalently to MOFs were capable of being recycled up to 12 times with negligible loss in their activity (Tudisco et al., 2016). However, this type of composites was seen to suffer from reduced sensitivity (e.g., relative to the one based on physical adsorption method). Such phenomenon was suspected to reflect lower number of enzyme/biomolecules in the composites due to inherent property of the reaction between the functional groups of MOFs and enzyme/biomolecules or the inefficiency of the catalyst/coupling agent employed during covalent attachment reaction. This drawback can nonetheless be overcome by selecting the efficient coupling agent for the aforementioned reaction or by incorporating the suitable functional groups in MOF by postsynthetic approach to facilitate effective coupling with enzymes.
## 4.2. Electrochemical sensing platform
Electrochemical biosensors have drawn increasing interest in recent decades due to their ability to monitor for target analytes to cope with current societal needs such as food safety, quality control, environmental monitoring, and disease diagnostics (Konvalina and Haick, 2014; Shehada et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2014a). Electrochemical biosensors using biomolecules as recognition elements offer some advantages such as fast response, sensitivity, selectivity, and ease of miniaturization (Hartmann and Kostrov, 2013). As discussed in earlier sections, these biomolecules lack operation stability, so they require immobilization with a suitable matrix to increase their operational stability under harsh reaction conditions. Especially in the case of electrochemical biosensors, the biocatalytic activity of the electrode surface and the mass transfer ability of the analyte entirely depend on the interfacial properties of the biorecognition material and type of host matrix used. MOFs have been proven as suitable matrices for immobilizing host materials and various biomolecules, which can be employed as electrode materials in the production of electrochemical biosensors.
The ZIF MOFs and dehydrogenase based electrochemical platform was used to develop a sensor for in-vivo monitoring of glucose (Ma et al., 2013). The different ZIF analogues (i.e., ZIF-7, ZIF-8, ZIF-67, ZIF68, and ZIF-70) were used as a substrate for co-immobilizing of two electro catalysts: 1) methylene green (MG) and 2) glucose dehydrogenase (GDH). These ZIFs are found to possess different porous, morphological, and surface characteristics. These authors selected ZIFs as immobilizing agents for enzymes during electrocatalysts in light of their advanced properties: (i) easy surface/physical adsorption of electrocatalysts; (ii) electrostatic interactions between functional groups of ZIFs and enzymes, and (iii) the high chemical stability of the ZIFs (Ma et al., 2013). Among different ZIFs, ZIF-70 was used by the authors for glucose sensing studies due to its superior adsorption abilities for MG and GDH (Ma et al., 2013). The adsorption capacities (mg/ g) for MG, when measured using diverse ZIFs, were estimated as 5.65 (ZIF-7), 6.79 (ZIF-8), 6.98 (ZIF-67), 3.82 (ZIF-68), 7.11 (ZIF-70). Similarly, the adsorption capacities $\mathbf{\left(mg/g\right)}$ for GDH were 88.3 (ZIF-8), 110.4 (ZIF-67), 584.9 (ZIF-68), 441.4 (ZIF-70). The ZIF-70/MG/GDH biosensor, when employed for the detection of glucose in a continuousflow system, exhibited highly sensitive and selective performance. The selectivity of the sensor was tested with endogenous electroactive species in a cerebral system (Table 2). The sensor was successfully tested for nearly real time to obtain selective sensing of glucose in the dialysate samples from the guinea pigs brains (Ma et al., 2013). A copper based MOF $\mathrm{\left(Cu-H_{2}B D C\right.}$ (1, 4-benzenedicarboxylic acid)) tyrosinase (try) chitosan composite modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE) was investigated for electrochemical sensing of bisphenol A (BPA) as shown in Fig. 2 (Wang et al., 2015). The large surface area provided by the 3D structure of MOF was advantageous for adsorption of the enzyme (try) as well as detection of BPA with improved sensitivity. In addition, the MOF enhanced the availability of BPA to activate its reaction with tyrosinase on the electrode surface via $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions. The promising results were obtained by using proposed methodology for BPA detection in spiked samples. The recoveries of the sensor were in the range of $94.0\substack{-101.6\%}$ . In addition, the above nanocomposite was also investigated for use as an electrochemical sensor for other BPs, i.e., bisphenol B (BPB), bisphenol F (BPF), bisphenol E (BPE), and bisphenol Z (BPZ) (Lu et al., 2016). The sensitivity of the nanosensor towards these BPs displayed strong dependency on the logarithms of the n-octanol/water partition coefficients. On the other hand, nanocomposite exhibited insignificant response towards bisphenol AP (BPAP), bisphenol AF (BPAF), bisphenol S (BPS), and tetrabromobisphenol A, although BPS, BPAP, and BPAF have structures similar to those of the former BPs. From the experimental results, it was concluded that the substituent groups on the bisphenol structure affected the electrochemical response of the nanocomposite for the detection of different BPs. The oxidation of the available ortho hydroxyl groups on phenol by tyrosinase was dependent on the effect of the other substituents on phenol, which controls the electron density of the hydroxyl group of phenol. These authors also reported that the composite was not affected by the copresence of nine potential interfering metal ions (Table 1). The reasons for the aforementioned property are: (1) the exceptional film-forming capability of biopolymer chitosan film and extraordinary adsorption characteristics for diverse heavy metal ions (Liu et al., 2013b; Zhang et al., 2016) and (2) the small pore size distribution $({\sim}4.9\mathrm{\AA})$ of the ultra-stable Cu-MOF restricts the diffusion of large hydrated heavy metal ions (Lu et al., 2016).
The hemin as naturally occurring metalloporphyrin exhibits both catalytic activity as well as redox mediator. The catalytic activity is due to the peroxidase like activity while redox mediator properties were ascribed to the reversible redox reactions of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ couple (Zhang and Dasgupta, 1992). However, hemin possess some limitations as catalyst like relatively short lifetime and poor solubility in neutral/alkaline buffer solutions (Lai et al., 2011). In order to overcome these limitations, hemin can be immobilized in certain matrix to increase its catalytic properties. Some immobilizing agents reported for the enhancement of its catalytic properties includes polymeric films, zeolites, and sol-gels (Battioni et al., 1996; Holland et al., 1998; Nestler and Severin, 2001). However, these immobilizing materials suffer from low hemin loading and/or leaching of the loaded substrate during functional operations which can drastically decrease the sensitivity during electrochemical sensing.
In this regard, MOF can serve as an excellent immobilizing agent to immobilize the hemin molecule. A multifunctional bioconjugate material (Au/hemin@MOF–TBA II (thrombin binding aptamer)–GOx (glucose oxidase)) was explored for electrochemical sensing of thrombin (TB) (Xie et al., 2015). The stepwise assembly of the composite and composite based electrocatalytic detection principle for TB is given in Fig. 3. In the present aptasensor, Au/hemin $@$ MOF–TBA II–GOx bioconjugates serve as secondary bioconjugates. TB was sandwiched between the self-assembled amino-terminated TBA I on gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) modified electrode and the secondary conjugates. Glucose was oxidized into gluconic acid by GOx, resulting in the generation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The hemin $@$ MOFs electrocatalyze the generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which resulted in amplification of the electrochemical signal of hemin. Thus, synthesized hemin@MOFs served as a multifunctional material, especially for three different functions: 1) catalysts, 2) redox mediators, and 3) loading platforms for enzymes (Xie et al., 2015). Another $\mathrm{Pt-NPs/MOF/}$ GOD nanocomposite material was reported as an electrochemical biosensor for glucose (Patra et al., 2015). Due to the excellent electrocatalytic properties of Pt-NPs and biocompatibility of MIL-100 (Fe), the nanocomposite modified electrode exhibited excellent electro-catalytic performance for glucose detection. The Fe centers in MIL-100 (Fe) were responsible for the electro-catalytic oxidation properties of the sensor towards glucose. This is because of the intrinsic peroxidase activity of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ center in MOF (Patra et al., 2015). This was concluded by substitution of Fe by Cr and Al in the MOF. The MOFs with Cr and Al displayed lower sensitivity and an elevated response time for electrocatalytic oxidation of glucose compared to the MIL-100 (Fe). The activity of $\mathbf{GOx}$ in the composite was assessed by calculating the Michaelis–Menten constant $\mathrm{(K_{m})}$ . The value of $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ for immobilized GOx and free GOx was found to be 2.4 and $33\mathrm{-}110\mathrm{mM}$ , respectively. A low $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ value generally indicates that the enzyme has a high affinity towards the substrate (Swoboda and Massey, 1965). High sensitivity (e.g., low limit of detection (LOD)) and low $\mathtt{K}_{\mathtt{m}}$ values suggest that the $\mathbf{GOx}/$ MIL-100(Fe)/PtNP nanocomposite biosensor provided a biocompatible microenvironment for enzymatic biosensing of glucose (Patra et al., 2015). The GOx immobilized Cu-Hemin MOF composite $\mathrm{(GOx/Cu-}$ Hemin-MOF) based glucose sensor was reported to work on electrocatalytic oxidation of glucose (He et al., 2016). The structure of Cuhemin MOFs was estimated to be ball-flowerlike having hollow cages. Moreover, the Cu-hemin MOF exhibited large specific surface area with abundant mesopores. The presence of pores in Cu-hemin MOFs was suspected for possible accommodation of many GOx molecules. GOD/ Cu-hemin MOF/GCE exhibited better electro-catalytic properties than Cu-Hemin/GCE. The composite modified GCE exhibited exceptional selectivity towards glucose determination even in the presence of 10- times higher concentrations of other sugars (i.e., fructose, galactose, and mannose), ascorbic acid (AA), and uric acid (UA) than glucose. The sensor demonstrated good performance for the accurate determination of glucose in serum of diabetes mellitus patients. The level of glucose in a patient sample was found to be $8.3\mathrm{mM}$ , which was very close to the clinical results (He et al., 2016). The long-term stability and reproducibility were the main parameters to consider for the reliability of any biosensor. The long-term stability of biosensor discussed was retained about $99.6\%$ of original current after $48\mathrm{h}$ when stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; it was $87.5\%$ of original current after 30 days (He et al., 2016). Similarly, when the reproducibility of the system for glucose sensing was tested with five different electrodes, the RSD value was estimated as $4.27\%$ .
The MOF (Fe-TCPP (Fe (III) meso-5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin chloride) based DNA sensor was developed by encapsulating HKUST-1(Cu)) and hairpin DNA allosteric switch (Ling et al., 2015). A functional MOF was synthesized by sequential conjugation with recognition element i.e., streptavidin (SA). The electrochemical signal read out was generated during the formation of 2,2- diaminoazobenzene from $o$ -phenylenediamine oxidation, mimetically catalyzed by FeTCPP $@$ MOF composites. These authors compared the catalytic activity of the FeTCPP $@$ MOF composites for said the aforementioned reaction with the naturally occurring enzyme HRP under different temperature and $\mathtt{p H}$ conditions. At $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , HRP lost $50\%$ catalytic activity whereas little activity was FeTCPP ${\ @\mathrm{MOF}}$ composites exhibited insignificant decreases. Similarly, when HRP was incubated in $\mathsf{p H9}$ buffer solution, its catalytic activity was ceased to $39\%$ . In contrast, the catalytic properties of the FeTCPP@MOF composites remained almots unchanged. This comparison shows the potential of composite materials under various conditions which can actually enable them to be used under diverse catalytic conditions (Ling et al., 2015). The formation of SA aptamer due to target DNA triggered allosteric switch was attributed for enhanced electrochemical signals. Thus, the specific recognition between aptamer structure and SA resulted in bringing the FeTCPP@MOF-SA probe close to the electrode (Ling et al., 2015). These MOF-based electrochemical sensors possessed high selectivity even towards a single mismatched DNA. The developed sensor system was investigated for the determination of target DNA in spiked human serum sams and had a recovery value of $97.0~\pm~1.3\%$ (Ling et al., 2015). In another report, a HP3 (labeled hairpin probes 3)/ AuNPs/MOF (Cu-2-aminoterptahlic acid) composite based ratiometric electrochemical aptasensor was developed for lipopolysaccharide (LPS) detection (Shen et al., 2015). The AuNP/Cu-MOF composites were able to serve as a nanocarrier for immobilizing HP3 as well as electroactive materials for signal reporting. The aptasensor exhibited specificity towards LPS in the presence of human serum albumin (HSA), procalcitonin (PCT), and C-reactive protein (CRP) (Shen et al., 2015). In that study, AuNP/Cu-MOF composites not only served as immobilizing matrix for large number of HP3 but also assisted as electroactive materials for signal report. These composites also facilitated catalyzing oxidation of glucose for enzyme-free signal amplification.
The electrochemical aptasensor was fabricated for LPS detection based on AuNP/MOFs (Ce-2-aminotepthalic acid) and $\scriptstyle2\ n^{2+}$ dependent DNAzyme-assisted re-cycling as dual signal amplifiers (Shen et al.,
2016). Instead of protein enzymes, these authors used DNAzymes because the latter has several advantages (e.g., chemical stability, cost effective, non-toxic, and facile synthesis). AuNP/Ce-MOFs AuNPs acted as nanocarriers for capturing -SH terminated hairpin probes 2 (HP2) to act as HP2/AuNP/Ce-MOF signal probes and Ce-MOFs assisted in oxidation of AA for signal amplification. Such NP embedded MOFs showed catalytic activity in the oxidation reaction of ascorbic acid (AA). The aptasensor exhibited specificity for LPS over PCT, CRP, and HSA. The pattern of signals exhibited by LPS with the aptasensor was unaltered in the presence of PCT, CRP, and HAS. In addition, the intensities of the signal change of PCT, CRP, and HAS analytes with the aptasensor were similar to blank. These proteins were also tested as interfering molecules during LPS sensing and did not have a significant effect on the performance of the sensor when tested with $10\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ LPS. The aptasensor exhibited good recoveries (93.6 to $104.4\%$ ) when tested for the sensing of LPS in human serum (Shen et al., 2016). The multifunctional Ag-NP/MOF composite material $\mathbf{Ag}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ -TSA has also been reported for electrochemical sensing of hydrogen peroxide, nitrite, and glucose (Dong et al., 2016).
The presence of TSA in Zn-TSA is found to be helpful in accelerating the interfacial electron transfer due to its delocalized electrons. Similarly, the presence of functional groups, i.e., carboxyl and thiol in TSA can facilitate the bioconjugation of a large number of proteins/ enzymes. The presence of Ag NPs in composites has several advantages such as improved mass/electron transfer, high effective/specific surface area, and promising electrical characteristics (Shahrokhian and Hosseini Nassab, 2013). Furthermore, this conjugate demonstrated easy transfer of interfacial electrons due to synergetic effect of the AgNPs and conjugate structure of TSA. Two types of carbon paste electrodes (CPEs) were developed by immobilizing myoglobin and GOx on CPEs modified with Ag $@2\mathbf{n}$ -TSA/ionic liquid. The former electrode was used for electrochemical sensing of hydrogen peroxide and nitrite, whereas glucose was monitored with the second modified electrode. These two composite modified electrodes possessed good electro-catalytic properties for all of their targets.
Electrochemiluminescence (ECL) immunosensors are another type of MOF based electrochemical sensor used for the determination of diverse analytes. For instance, MOF (MIL-101) CdSe quantum dot (QD) nano composite (MIL-101/CdSe/GCE) based sensors have been used for ECL derived detection of carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) (Liu et al., 2017). The hydrothermally synthesized CdSe QD/MIL-101 composite was deposited on GCE for the sensing of immunogens. The as-prepared MIL-101/CdSe/GCE exhibited high ECL activity and selectivity towards CEA due to the presence of QDs and selective adsorption and efficient accumulation of CEA by MIL-101. The developed ECL immunosensor was tested for its selective response by using interfering molecules (such as human IL-6, BSA, and their mixture). A relative signal deviation of $1.46{-}3.46\%$ was observed when the performance of the sensor was investigated in the presence of interfering molecule concentrations of $10^{-6}{-}10^{-3}\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . A MOFs and GOx based multi-enzyme system was employed for the electrochemical determination of glucose was fabricated by combing (Wang et al., 2016). The composite structure fabricated with the help of polydopamine (PDA) and ZIF-8 on $\mathsf{C a C O}_{3}$ templates served as the mimetic enzymes as well as biocompatible and non-toxic microenvironment providers. To improve the electron transfer between composite and electrode, reduced graphene oxide (rGO) was attached to the PDA/ZIF-8 via $\scriptstyle{\mathfrak{\pi}}-{\mathfrak{\pi}}$ interactions and hydrogen bonding. Accordingly, the developed PDA/ZIF- $8@\mathrm{rGO}$ exhibited excellent electro-catalytic activity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Furthermore, the GOx enzyme was immobilized on the as-prepared PDA/ZIF- $8@\mathbf{rGO}$ microcapsule to develop mimetic multi-enzyme platform, i.e., GOx/PDA/ZIF$8@{\bf{r G O}}/{\bf{G C E}}$ for glucose biosensor. The microcapsule based glucose biosensor also exhibited good selectivity in the analysis of glucose in real samples.
Microperoxidase-11 (MP-11) encapsulated in MOF PCN (porous coordination network)-333 (Al) was grown on a three-dimensional (3D)
kenaf stem-derived porous carbon (3D–KSC) for the development of a hydrogen peroxide biosensor (Gong et al., 2017). The ball-flower-like PCN-333 (Al) with mesopores of $5.5\mathrm{nm}$ was especially suitable for encapsulating MP-11 $(1.1\times1.7\times3.3\mathrm{nm})$ . The results obtained confirmed that encapsulated MP-11 molecules showed better sensing performance than the free enzyme. The sensor exhibited selectivity towards hydrogen peroxide in the presence of different chemicals (Table 1). Glucose, AA, and UA interfered slightly with the detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (less than $5\%$ , whereas other components such as ${\mathrm{Na}}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , and cysteine did not exhibit obvious interference (Gong et al., 2017). In another report, an electrochemical DNA sensor was developed for detection of mutations in the FGFR3 gene (a pathogenic gene from achondroplasia) has been reported (Chen et al., 2017). It was noted that biotin-streptavidin system was suspected for the sensing signals in addition to the biocatalytic signal materials. Hemin-MOF/PtNPs were synthesized via a single step reduction in a beaker. The above composites amplified the signal due to their good conductivity as well as their catalyzing ability to oxidize $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . For further amplification of the sensor signals, the electrode was modified with several materials (i.e., rGO-tetra ethylene pentamine (rGO-TEPA), AuNP, and streptavidin). The aptasensor imparted good selectivity to the target DNA and has been applied to other oligonucleotides such as mismatch oligonucleotides (i.e., single-base, double-base, and three-base mismatch) and noncomplementary targets (random). The current response for oligonucleotides with a 10-fold higher concentration (than the target DNA) did not exhibit any current changes similar to the response of the blank. However, current signals increased significantly in the presence of target DNA. The authors tested the proposed DNA sensor to detect healthy maternal serum samples spiked with target DNA and obtained recovery values ranging from $97.4\%$ to $105.2\%$ (Chen et al., 2017).
Recently, ratiometric glucose biosensor was reported based on GOxAuNPs-Cu(BTC)MOF-3D KSCs (3dimensional macroporous carbon) integrated electrode (Song et al., 2017). This composite electrode was constructed as fallows. First, Cu-BTC was grown on a 3D KSCs integrated electrode. Then AuNPs were electrodeposited on to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathtt{B T C})$ MOF-3D KSCs integrated electrode. Finally, GOx was deposited on AuNPs-Cu(BTC)MOF-3D KSCs integrated electrode to generate ratiometric sensor for glucose. The present sensor system exhibited high accuracy, selectivity, and sensitivity towards glucose (Song et al., 2017).
The AuNPs based electrochemical biosensor was developed on paper working electrode (PWE) for the detection of ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ by using DNA modified iron-porphyrinic MOF ((Fe-P)n-MOF-Au-GR) hybrids as signal probes (Wang et al., 2017). The aforementioned sensing probe was selective for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in presence of many other competing metal ions. The AuNPs-MOF based sensing platform and sensing probes system exhibited the several advantages such as economical fabrication, simple construction, and high sensitivity/selectivity. These advantages demonstrated the great potentials of the developed system for the realtime ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ detection in environmental as well as biological samples (Wang et al., 2017). The analytical characteristics of the electrochemical biosensors are shown in Table 2.
In electrochemical sensing applications, the MOF–biomolecule composites exhibited enhanced performance in terms of sensitivity as well as selectivity as was the case of optical sensing systems. The MOFs increased the sensitivity during electrochemical sensing by two means: i) holding a large number of enzyme molecules through diverse interactions (like $\scriptstyle{\pi-\pi}$ interactions between aromatic groups of enzymes and organic linkers of MOF) and ii) The MOFs on the electrode surface effectively adsorbed the analytes from the bulk solution (preconcentration) through the $\pi\mathrm{-}\pi$ interactions between the aromatic groups of analytes and organic linkers of MOF (Kempahanumakkagari et al., 2018). Likewise, the MOFs will also help increase selectivity during electrochemical sensing for particular analyte with the aid of their particular pore size (Gkaniatsou et al., 2017). The MOFs do not adsorb the analytes with bigger size then their pore size from the bulk solution.
This property can help attain the selectivity while the enzyme has activity towards many substrates (analytes) present in the bulk solution. The pristine MOFs were also reported as catalyst due to the presence of acid/basic sites and redox groups (organic ligands). The selection of such MOFs as host materials for enzymes can result in higher sensitivity. This may in fact reflect the synergetic effect drawn by both the MOF and enzymes present in the composites.
## 4.3. Biological sensing platform
Recently, biomolecule-embedded MOFs or bio-MOFs have also been recommended as biosensors due to their potential applicability in various biological systems. Some current studies have examined the possible biomedical efficacy of porous bio-MOFs. In general, the Lanthanide-based MOFs were used extensively as multimodal cellular probes. Nevertheless, for biosensing applications, both sensing characteristics as well as biocompatibility of the MOF materials are important. Certain MOFs may have associated with the disadvantage of toxic metal ion leaching in combination with harmful components. In this regard, construction of bio-MOFs that can integrate biomolecules and biocompatible metals ions (e.g., $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ) in their structures is very demanding (An et al., $2009\mathrm{a})$ . For instance, R-UiO (where $\mathbb{R}=\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{n}\cdot$ [10,20 diphenyl-5,15-di(p-benzoato)-porphyrin]) bio-MOF was fabricated as a phosphorescence/fluorescence dual-emissive material for intracellular $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing (Xu et al., 2016). As $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ is a very crucial component in aerobic respiration that provides metabolic energy to cells, reduction in the levels of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ can cause various diseases, including vascular disease, pulmonary disease, and even cancer. Consequently, monitoring and quantifying $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ levels in living cells are of great significance especially for cancer diagnosis, tumor pathophysiology assessment, and assessment of the therapeutic effects of anticancer treatments. The R-UiO based bio-MOF contains a Pt(II)-porphyrin ligand (Pt-5,15-di(p-benzoato)porphyrin (DBP-Pt)), which acts as the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitive probe. Conversely, the rhodamine B isothiocyanate ligand in this aforementioned MOF behaves as an $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -insensitive reference probe. The R-UiO bio-MOF demonstrates good crystallinity, higher stability, and an exceptional ratiometric luminescence response to $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ partial pressure. Moreover, in vitro experiments in mouse colon carcinoma CT26 cells evidently confirmed the applicability of R-UiO as an intracellular biosensor for $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ .
In vivo biosensing has the potential to modernize health care by empowering personalized medicine. Simple implantation of a sensor into a human/animal body could transmit clinically relevant health information on a continuous basis. Through constant monitoring with a sensor, an individual's baseline health can be assumed. On the basis of this assumption, therapeutic drug monitoring could be possible by offering an individualized report on the pharmacokinetics of a drug. In this regard, one interesting recent study on enzyme embedded MOFs has been published by Cheng et al. (2016). An in vivo microdialysis approach was employed in their study. The authors combined hemin (peroxidase mimic) and glucose oxidase inside a zeolitic imidazolate MOF (ZIF-8) (He et al., 2014b) and demonstrated that the proximity of the two enzyme systems allowed sensing of glucose with fast kinetics. Moreover, the glucose in the cerebral of the living rats were also visualized by enzyme embedded MOF (Cheng et al., 2016).
The efficiency of glucose oxidase and hemin containing ZIF-8 as the INAzyme was thoroughly investigated. Initially, the glucose oxidase catalyzed the oxidation between glucose and dioxygen to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Successively, the nearby hemin catalyzed the oxidation of a peroxidase with the newly formed in situ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce detection signals. Discernable green color appeared after the incubation (at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and addition of INAzyme into the solution of glucose and chromogenic substrate (ABTS). Such coloring signified that cascade biocatalysis should take place as assumed. Comparison was also made using the pristine ZIF-8, only hemin embedded ZIF-8, and glucose oxidase embedded ZIF-8. However, color change was no longer observed. These results considerably confirmed the inevitability of assimilation for both glucose oxidase and hemin inside the ZIF-8 framework for the intended cascade reactions. Nevertheless, this type of in vivo biosensor study demands future developments, as currently there are only a limited number of researchers who have tested biosensors in vivo due to inherent fragility, irreversible binding, non-selectivity, and/or difficulty in use.
Another biocompatible iron based porphyrin MOF nanosheets physically adsorbed with heparin specific AG73 peptides were used for in vivo monitoring of heparin activity in live rats (Cheng et al., 2017). The MOF nanosheets are made up of $\scriptstyle2\ n^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ ions along with heme-like metalloporphyrin polydentate organic ligands like TCPP (Fe)). The MOF nanosheets can mimic the peroxidase activity due to the presence of the above ligands. These composites exhibited high specificity towards heparin compared to its analogues and other endogenous biomolecules. The AG73 peptides present on MOF nanosheets were seen to block the activity from other substrate-catalyst reactions (Cheng et al., 2017). Furthermore, these MOF composites were used for online monitoring of elimination process of heparin in live rat on quantitative basis by coupling with micro dialysis technique. These MOF-based nanozymes found some advantages over their bulk analogues such as: (i) Large exposed surface with numerous accessible active sites for enzymatic catalysis and (ii) Large number of binding sites to interact with target compounds.
Recently, GOx-MOF composite (GOx-ZIF-8) was used as signal transduction unit via biomimetic mineralization process for the construction of fluorescence immunosensor for galectin-4 (Zhang et al., 2017). This galectin-4 is found to be useful as a biomarker for different kinds of cancers. The developed fluorescence immunosensor was tested for monitoring galectin-4 in real cancerous samples (like lysate); accordingly, the sensing system was able to detect target proteins in cell lysates (Zhang et al., 2017).
## 4.4. Miscellaneous sensing platform
The entrapment of biomolecules, organic linkers, metal nodes, and nanostructures in the high-surface-area matrices of the MOFs has facilitated their implementation in various sensing applications including pH sensing, lanthanide cation sensing, and temperature sensing. Such incorporation of biomolecules in MOFs imparted direct interaction with analyte and hence increased the sensitivity and selectivity of the sensor. It is essential to address that the arena of MOFs for miscellaneous sensing is still immature compared to the other sensor materials. For example, Kahn et al. (2017) have recently published an article on the design of stimuli-responsive DNA-modified MOFs. They synthesized pH- and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ -ion-responsive DNA-functionalized MOFs that exhibited switchable release of loads entrapped within the MOF structures (Kahn et al., 2017). To accomplish the switchable release study, they implemented i-motif and triplex DNA nanostructures for the fabrication of pH-responsive MOFs as well as a $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ stabilized G-quadruplex as a functional ion-gating unit. These authors selected rhodamine 6G which can maintain pH-independent fluorescence within a pH range of 5.0–8.0. Thus, they utilized fluorescence as a probe to monitor release of the load. Their experimental results clearly indicated that pH was the main stimuli behind the release of the loaded rhodamine 6G from the DNA embedded MOFs through a ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{G}}{{\bullet}{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}$ triplex structure unlocking mechanism. The results also indicated that other stimuli such as metalion/ligand, strand/anti-strand, or light may be applied to accelerate the controlled release of loads from MOFs. The characteristics and functions of such materials are expected to have an impact on the advancement of sensors, photonic devices, new catalysts, and drug delivery carriers.
The extraordinary characteristics of well-defined MOF structures can be improved further through the incorporation of sensitizers, i.e., chromophoric sensitizers and lanthanide cations. The fundamental aspect of such incorporation resulted in an increment in the number of emitted photons to further enhance the detection sensitivity. Considering this fact, metal-adeninate bio-MOFs has been developed by a few researchers (An et al., $2009\mathrm{a};$ ; Shen and Yan, 2015a) for hosting visible as well as NIR-emitting lanthanide cations. For instance, An et al. (2011) used nitrate salts of lanthanide cations including samarium $(\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{3+})$ , terbium $(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+})$ , europium ${(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+})}$ , or ytterbium $(\mathrm{Y}\mathsf{b}^{3+})$ for the incorporation of lanthanide ions into bio-MOF-1 pores. Upon excitation of bio-MOF- $1365\mathrm{nm}$ , orange-pink, red, and green colors were emitted, respectively by samarium, europium, and terbium. The emitted colors can be used for the qualitative lanthanide sensitization in aqueous media. This finding established that a bio-MOF scaffold can efficiently sensitize four lanthanide cations. Out of four lanthanide cations, three displayed signals in visible light, while one lanthanide is emitted in NIR-range emitting lanthanide cation in an aqueous environment (An et al., 2011). Note that only one accepting electronic level in ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ makes it unique lanthanide cation for energy transfer. This work is an exceptional case where a bio-MOF sensitizes lanthanide cations that can emit NIR in an aqueous medium for the first time. The ytterbium-embedded bio-MOF was subsequently utilized as a probe for gaseous $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing. The response of the above-mentioned bio-MOF to $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ gas was investigated by measuring the fluorescence signal intensity of the ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ at $970\mathrm{nm}$ ( $\mathsf{a t}340\ \mathrm{nm}$ light excitation). A similar type assembly of a sensing system composed of bio-MOF-1 and lanthanide ions has been designed by Wu and Yan (2016). These authors prepared hybrid MOFs (HBN-Eu-bio-MOF-1, HBN-Ln-bio-MOF-1, where $\mathrm{HBN}=6$ -hydroxybenz[de]anthracen-7-one) through ion-exchange/coordination post synthesis method. Among these MOFs, the HBN-Eu-bioMOF-1 was selected for the sensing application as its luminescent properties were highly sensitive towards both $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions (especially for $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}.$ ). Moreover, this luminescence-based sensing was also highly sensitive for acetone molecules (Wu and Yan, 2016).
The polymerization reaction was employed for the synthesis of photofunctional polymer hybrid thin films (Shen and Yan, 2015c). The fabrication of these films are based on bio-MOFs (zinc complexes bioMOF-1 $\mathrm{(Zn_{8}(a d)_{4}(B P D C)6O\cdot2M e_{2}N H_{2}))}$ and rare earth complexes RE (BPDC)(Ad) (BPDC $\mathbf{\tau}=$ biphenyl-4, $4^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylic acid, ${\bf A d}={\bf\Gamma}$ adenine). On the other hand, two organic linkers (i.e., 4-Vinylpyridine and ethyl methacrylate) were used to help in the coordination between rare earth ions with the bio-MOF $(\Upsilon^{3+},\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ , and ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ ) during the copolymerization reaction. Under excitation at different wavelengths, these bioMOF polymer thin films displayed multi-color luminescence in visible region due to the existence of diverse rare earth ions. Thin films doped with ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ emitted white light when excited with ${329}\mathrm{nm}$ light source, which can be exploited in optical device fabrications. Apart from optical sensing, this rare earth doped thin film promisingly acts as a novel luminescent thermometer as its color changing property distinctly varies with temperature. The film displayed luminescence color from blue-green to pink with temperature change from 100 to $320\mathrm{K}$ . Thus, such films can be employed for the determination of temperature. The temperature sensing ability of this innovative thin film may open a new avenue for further application of bio-MOF hybrid materials in diverse fields.
## 5. Conclusions and future perspectives
In conclusion, biomolecule-embedded MOFs have been demonstrated to be an innovative sensing platform for various applications. Although the practical application of biomolecule-embedded MOF supports is still in its early stage, the design and industrial applications of MOFs with considerably advanced properties (e.g., larger pore sizes, non-toxic nature, and functionalities) are expected to grow further. As discussed in this review, some key challenges should be resolved to facilitate real world sensing applications of bio-MOF hybrid materials. Nevertheless, the extension of research in this field of science is expected to stimulate further progress in the design and synthesis of novel MOFs for a better acquisition of knowledge regarding various types of interactions between MOFs and biomolecules.
Biocatalytic or biomolecule-embedded supports are a new emerging area of research that demands scaling-up of lab-level processes to largescale (industrial) operations. The development of MOF-based innovative platforms, if realized in more eco-friendly ways, is expected to extend many important biomedical applications including therapeutics and diagnostics fields. Because of the unique and versatile nature of MOFs, extensive efforts are expected to be devoted towards the development of a highly advanced functional system for various sensing applications based on their substrates in the near future.
